1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,035
In this lesson, we'll locate points by
distance and angle instead of x and y.

2
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:11,052
A polar point r, theta means: turn by
theta, then go out r units.

3
00:00:11,502 --> 00:00:16,345
The point two, pi over three is two units
out along the sixty degree ray.

4
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:22,871
Each point has infinitely many polar
names. Adding two pi to theta gives the

5
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:23,653
same point.

6
00:00:24,103 --> 00:00:30,309
A negative r means go the opposite way:
negative two, pi over three is the same as

7
00:00:30,309 --> 00:00:32,125
two, four pi over three.

8
00:00:33,267 --> 00:00:39,731
To convert to rectangular, x equals r
cosine theta and y equals r sine theta.

9
00:00:40,181 --> 00:00:44,298
Four, five pi over six becomes negative
two root three, two.

10
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:51,448
To go back, r equals the square root of x
squared plus y squared, and tangent theta

11
00:00:51,448 --> 00:00:54,564
equals y over x, with the quadrant
checked.

12
00:00:55,014 --> 00:01:01,169
Negative one, negative one has r equals
root two and theta equals five pi over

13
00:01:01,169 --> 00:01:01,563
four.

14
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:09,441
Equations convert too. r equals four sine
theta: multiply by r to get r squared

15
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:11,575
equals four r sine theta.

16
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:18,599
So x squared plus y squared equals four y,
a circle of radius two centered at zero,

17
00:01:18,599 --> 00:01:18,915
two.

18
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:25,324
Your turn. Convert zero, negative three to
polar form with r positive.

19
00:01:25,774 --> 00:01:27,310
Pause the video and try it.

20
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:30,491
r is three and theta is three pi over two.

21
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:35,985
In this lesson, we'll draw the beautiful
curves that polar equations make.

22
00:01:37,133 --> 00:01:43,358
r equals three is a circle of radius
three. Theta equals pi over four is a line

23
00:01:43,358 --> 00:01:44,856
through the origin.

24
00:01:45,306 --> 00:01:51,070
r equals four cosine theta is a circle
through the origin, centered on the x

25
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:51,450
axis.

26
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,781
To graph, make a table: choose theta
values, compute r, and plot.

27
00:01:57,231 --> 00:02:04,564
For r equals two plus two cosine theta: at
zero, r is four. At pi over two, two. At

28
00:02:04,564 --> 00:02:05,359
pi, zero.

29
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:13,066
That curve is a cardioid, a heart shape.
In general, r equals a plus b cosine theta

30
00:02:13,066 --> 00:02:14,094
is a limacon.

31
00:02:14,544 --> 00:02:19,686
When a equals b, it's a cardioid. When a
is less than b, it has an inner loop.

32
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:23,756
r equals a cosine of n theta is a rose.

33
00:02:24,206 --> 00:02:30,549
If n is odd, it has n petals. If n is
even, it has two n petals. r equals three

34
00:02:30,549 --> 00:02:33,038
sine two theta has four petals.

35
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:39,593
Check symmetry to save work. Replacing
theta with negative theta tests symmetry

36
00:02:39,593 --> 00:02:41,036
about the polar axis.

37
00:02:41,486 --> 00:02:46,862
Cosine graphs are always symmetric about
the x axis, since cosine is even.

38
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,928
Your turn. How many petals does r equals
five cosine three theta have?

39
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:54,914
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:02:55,364 --> 00:02:57,902
n equals three is odd, so three petals.

41
00:02:59,033 --> 00:03:04,430
In this lesson, we'll describe curves by
giving x and y each as a function of a

42
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:05,454
third variable.

43
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:12,040
In parametric equations, x and y both
depend on a parameter, usually t.

44
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:17,780
As t changes, the point x of t, y of t
traces a path with a direction.

45
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:25,108
Take x equals t plus one and y equals t
squared, for t from negative two to two.

46
00:03:25,558 --> 00:03:32,087
t equals negative two gives negative one,
four. t equals zero gives one, zero. t

47
00:03:32,087 --> 00:03:34,454
equals two gives three, four.

48
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:42,426
To find the ordinary equation, eliminate
t. From x equals t plus one, t equals x

49
00:03:42,426 --> 00:03:43,280
minus one.

50
00:03:43,730 --> 00:03:47,015
So y equals x minus one squared, a
parabola.

51
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:54,576
x equals three cosine t, y equals three
sine t, for t from zero to two pi.

52
00:03:55,026 --> 00:04:01,146
Since cosine squared plus sine squared is
one, x squared plus y squared equals nine:

53
00:04:01,146 --> 00:04:03,623
a circle, traced counterclockwise.

54
00:04:04,766 --> 00:04:08,990
Different parametrizations can trace the
same path in different ways.

55
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:15,540
x equals three cosine two t, y equals
three sine two t goes around the same

56
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:17,248
circle twice as fast.

57
00:04:18,366 --> 00:04:23,998
Your turn. Eliminate the parameter: x
equals two t, y equals t plus three.

58
00:04:24,448 --> 00:04:25,984
Pause the video and try it.

59
00:04:26,434 --> 00:04:30,936
t equals x over two, so y equals x over
two plus three.

60
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:36,653
In this lesson, we'll use parametric
equations to track moving objects.

61
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:42,578
A boat starts at two, one and moves with
velocity three, four per hour.

62
00:04:43,028 --> 00:04:48,575
Its position is x equals two plus three t,
y equals one plus four t.

63
00:04:49,699 --> 00:04:56,550
A ball launched at speed v at angle theta
from height h follows x equals v cosine

64
00:04:56,550 --> 00:05:03,570
theta times t, and y equals h plus v sine
theta times t minus sixteen t squared, in

65
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:03,993
feet.

66
00:05:04,443 --> 00:05:09,520
Horizontal motion is steady; vertical
motion is pulled down by gravity.

67
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:16,737
Launch at eighty feet per second at thirty
degrees from the ground: x equals about

68
00:05:16,737 --> 00:05:21,994
sixty nine point three t, and y equals
forty t minus sixteen t squared.

69
00:05:22,444 --> 00:05:28,001
The top of the arc is at t equals forty
over thirty two, one point two five

70
00:05:28,001 --> 00:05:30,743
seconds, where y is twenty five feet.

71
00:05:31,866 --> 00:05:36,559
It lands when y equals zero: t equals two
point five seconds.

72
00:05:37,009 --> 00:05:42,439
The horizontal distance is sixty nine
point three times two point five, about

73
00:05:42,439 --> 00:05:44,625
one hundred seventy three feet.

74
00:05:45,766 --> 00:05:52,410
A fence ten feet tall is one hundred fifty
feet away. When x is one fifty, t is about

75
00:05:52,410 --> 00:05:54,598
two point one seven seconds.

76
00:05:55,048 --> 00:05:59,101
Then y is about eleven point six feet. It
clears the fence.

77
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:06,086
Your turn. A ball is launched straight up:
y equals sixty four t minus sixteen t

78
00:06:06,086 --> 00:06:08,062
squared. When does it land?

79
00:06:08,512 --> 00:06:10,048
Pause the video and try it.

80
00:06:10,498 --> 00:06:16,151
sixteen t times four minus t equals zero,
so t equals four seconds.

